there are images of police using  violence against peaceful protesters  there are images of police creating  confrontations even attacking but  defenseless elderly people we played a  video of police knocking over an elderly  man with a cane shooting at the media  these images have gone viral  over the weekend now I want to be really  clear I already did a story about how  what's going on in America right now is  not just about one thing it's not just  about race it's not only about police  it's not exclusively about capitalism or  Trump this is a massive multifaceted  ordeal but that doesn't mean that we  shouldn't look at individual elements  and one of these elements is the idea of  police as oppressors versus police's  victims as some have started framing  police to be we are seeing wholesale  pepper spraying of peaceful protesters  we've seen members of the media shot  with rubber bullets we saw kids hit with  pepper spray we've seen police ramming  police vehicles into crowds a Dallas  woman was hit in the face with a rubber  bullet while walking home with her  groceries police wildly hitting people  with batons in some cases people walking  away from protests none of this excuses  any violent actions by protesters and  there have been horrible actions  including attacking business owners  beating one business owner to within an  inch of his life for trying to defend  his business beating a female business  owner brutally out in front of her store  we have seen it all but I want to focus  in a little bit here on the police  component and talk about it with you  there are people and I'm friends with  some of them on Facebook I'm seeing it  on Twitter there are people who have  immediately taken to painting police's  victims arguments like a bad cop may  have killed George Floyd in Minnesota  but now innocent police all over the  place are being attacked for no reason  and often these arguments end up in  debates about numbers like well most  police are good ways it 97 percent or 99  percent well is it 99 or ninety nine  point three percent and I said earlier  in the show imagine we  out with some precision what portion of  police are the problem is we might call  it and what portion of police are not  imagine ninety nine point two percent of  police are good okay  well I imagine that most of my audience  even if you're okay with property crime  in protests where insurance companies  are the ones taking a hit in  corporations I imagine most of my  audience is not okay with unprovoked  attacks against random police officers  who aren't doing anything wrong if you  do believe that for what happened to  George Floyd in Minnesota it's okay to  just physically attack an officer in  Miami we're just not on the same page I  think that's the minority of my audience  does our analysis depend though on  whether it's ninety nine point two or  ninety nine point three percent of  police that are good guys in it for the  right reasons because what really this  is about is context and power dynamics  in total once you concede that most cops  are good and doing their job as best  they can often in difficult situations  sometimes dangerous situations you also  have some cops that go into law  enforcement specifically because they  would love an opportunity to beat the  crap out of someone just like some  portion of people go into the military  because they like the idea of shooting  someone and killing them in some kind of  a theater of war at some point that is  some percentage okay so so then what  right whether it's 0.8 or 0.7 or 0.08  percent of police that are going in  because they want to do violence and  misbehave and have all the worst  intentions what next and that's where  the more difficult conversations start  questions like are the good cops who are  just doing their job part of the problem  in that if you're not part of the  solution are you implicitly supporting  the status quo of that small percentage  of police that do horrible things and  get away with it  to what degree should every quote good  cop quit in protest to show solidarity  with the protests leaving our country to  be policed only by the bad cops  what can  as a sign of being complicit is silence  a sign of being complicit  what power and responsibility do  individual police officers have to fix  the problem versus police chiefs versus  federal law enforcement agencies versus  versus police unions that represent  officers around the country should the  good cops be treated respectfully or  suspiciously absent any reason to  believe they are one of the bad cops  how should they be treated by protesters  the average person etc when you start  asking these questions you realize that  most of the changes that need to be made  are systemic changes it needs to be much  more likely that if you do the wrong  thing as a police officer you will be  caught and punished it's not likely  right now in a lot of our society just  from this weekend  we have dozens if not hundreds of  officers on video doing horrible things  are we confident they're going to be  identified and investigated and charged  and punished when it's appropriate not  really because if it were more likely  that they were held accountable they  would probably change their behavior  that is a systemic issue a good system  will take care of that sliver of police  that are doing SiC horrible things so  let's answer the questions one by one  first of all being a police officer is  not like being an accountant okay  accountants don't represent communities  in the way that police officers do  accountants don't wield their power with  firearms and accountants don't have a  power dynamic relative to their clients  the way police officers have a power  dynamic a relative to the people that  they are dealing with so on the one hand  the good police officers must say much  more loudly these things are not okay  and we're committed to fixing it and  changing it and good for them many  police officers have done exactly that  at the same time the anger by protesters  towards a system of policing that is  fundamentally broken in many parts of  our country is justified anger and this  is why it's so powerful when police  officers get it and say I'm with you  like the  sheriff in Michigan who marched with  protesters and the video going viral and  it's important for protesters to know  that their anger with the system is  justified and yet it's not okay to go  attack random individual cops who have  not done anything wrong which is why it  was great to see protesters protecting a  police officer Saturday night who had  done nothing wrong and was separated  from his squad this is why it was great  to see video from I believe it was  Brooklyn last night with organizers  saying do not interact with the police  we are not here for that we are here to  get our message out do not incite  violence if you see anyone inciting  violence call them out and you know in  the midst of this there's this really  ugly thing that's going on where there  are people sort of making the focus that  the protesters are from out of town and  at one point there was some wrong report  that more than half of protesters in  some place were from out of town  another report it said it seemed like  one out of seven were from out of town  now sometimes that could mean the next  town over it's not really a big deal but  the point is this out of town narrative  is being used to imply that you merely  have people from other communities who  are not coming in to protest because  they actually are concerned about police  brutality and relations or whatever but  that they are coming in to provoke or to  loot or to steal you could be concerned  about what went on and drive from  another state and it also appears to be  completely untrue so we've got to be  really understanding of how some of  these narratives are being used to  distort and discredit real justifiable  anger we also have to recognize the  growing number of protests that are  saying we are not here to do violence at  all and we are not here to do violence  to the police but we have to realize  that the anger is justified given the  systemic dynamics that exist that's sort  of my sense of it right now I'm glad to  hear from you about it as well
 

